            <ArticleTitle>Novel &quot;crowbar effect'' approach to improve success rate of recanalization of coronary chronic total occlusions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S223-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3233/THC-150957</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Coronary chronic total occlusions (CTO) are the most challenging lesions to treat percutaneously. Thus, consistent efforts are made to develop new approaches to treat CTO.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the key points of a novel ``crowbar effect'' approach to improve the success rate of recanalization of CTOs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and fifty-seven patients with CTO were treated with PCI using the regular antegrade guide wire approach. Of them, 36 patients (22.9%) showed that while the first guide wire was inserted into the CTO lesions, a small balloon had difficulty passing through the CTO lesions. For those patients, the new crowbar effect technique was used to allow the balloon to pass through the lesions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The coronary CTO vessels in 35 patients (97.2%) were completely opened. Coronary perforation occurred in 5 patients (13.8%). This perforation was properly treated and did not lead to serious complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The crowbar effect technique proved successful as an alternative antegrade method for opening CTO. The procedure of this novel method is easy to accomplish and success rates are high.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
